// Adopting on a repository that already shipped.
//
// The obvious move is to transcribe: the capability docs become capability records, the
// release history becomes releases, the ADRs become decisions. Every one of those artifacts
// makes claims, and transcribing them imports the claims WITHOUT their evidence — so the
// record looks checked from its first commit and is not. That is the trust-transfer problem
// arriving through the front door of the tool built to close it.
//
// This repository ran the experiment on itself and did not mean to. TS.260821.06 ported
// three Markdown capability records:
//
//   two ported, and GAINED four gate tests and a keeps-consistent each — neither of which
//     the Markdown originals declared, because a Markdown capability record declares no
//     gate test it can fail
//   one did not port at all. CAP.method-spine-and-execution had been hollow since the graph
//     replaced the spine, and nothing said so for two versions
//
// The port was slower than a transcription and it found a dead capability. That is the whole
// argument, and it was made by accident.

thin-slice "TS.260821.17" {
    slug "adopt-without-transcribing"
    title "The record contains what it relies on, and cites nothing it does not"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.delivery-record"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    command "check"
    actor "somebody putting this method on a repository that already shipped"
    produces-event "ProvenanceDeclared" "TranscriptionRefused"
    needs-read-model "the-problem-as-it-stands"

    trigger "a repository with years of docs adopts the method, and the docs look like records"
    outcome "prior work informs the record and never becomes it, and where it informed something is visible"

    scenario #"""
        Given a repository holding capability documents, ADRs and release history
        When the method is adopted
        Then those artifacts are DISCOVERY DATA — stormed, walked, argued with — and the record
             is built new from what survives, because a claim transcribed is a claim imported
             without its evidence, and a record that looks checked from its first commit is
             worth less than an empty one.
        """#

    step "1" does="prior artifacts enter as evidence: a storm reads them, a walkthrough tests one path through them, and what survives is written fresh"
    step "2" does="what prior work TAUGHT is restated in the record's own words. Where it lived is not recorded at all — that pointer is guaranteed to rot, and a dead pointer outlives the file as a confident lie"
    step "3" does="a capability still passes its four gate tests on its own terms. A transcription cannot: the tests are what the source document never had"
    step "4" does="release history is read for what it CLAIMS and bound only where the record can compute it. S1 was withdrawn for exactly this and is the precedent"
    step "5" does="the same rule applies inward. An `evidence=` naming a test function is a citation the record cannot follow, and there are a hundred and thirty-five of them"
    step "6" does="a claim's evidence states WHAT was shown, not where. A renamed test then cannot silently orphan a claim, and the record says what it proved rather than where somebody can go to see it"

    excludes "a migration tool. Anything that reads an existing tree and writes records is a \
        transcription with better manners, and the artifact it produces is indistinguishable from \
        one somebody thought about"
    excludes "`informed-by` in any form. A path rots; a path marked as provenance rots while \
        looking deliberate. The substance is restated into the record and where it came from is \
        not recorded, because that is the half guaranteed not to survive"
    excludes "the record's VERIFIED assertions about the world. `doctrine-surface.path` names a \
        file and `a-declared-surface-ships` REFUSES when it is absent; `release.index.commit` \
        names a commit and the seal covers it; `publishes-to` is where the record WRITES rather \
        than what it reads. None of the three can rot silently, which is the property that makes a \
        citation dangerous. If that distinction is wrong the seventy-eight surfaces and the release \
        index are the next thing to rework, and this exclusion is where to argue with it"
    excludes "refusing an adoption that transcribes. The record already resists it — a copied \
        capability has no cluster, no gate tests and no exclusion, and the shape check says so. \
        A second rule would be enforcing what the schema already enforces"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="adopt-the-method says what to do with what is already there, which is the first question a brownfield adopter has"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="a field naming a file the checker cannot follow is refused, so nothing in the record can point outward and go stale unnoticed"
    layer "docs"        reaches="`what did this come from` is answerable for a record built on prior work"

    claim "C1" text="no record cites a path the checker cannot follow" \
        settled-by="a rule reporting any field whose value names a file outside the record, exercised over the whole tree — and the count taken to zero before it flips"
    claim "C2" text="a transcribed capability fails the shape check on its own, with no rule added" \
        settled-by="a test declaring a capability with a doing and a name and nothing else — the shape a Markdown record transcribes to — asserting it is refused for the cluster, the gate tests and the exclusion it does not have"
    claim "C4" text="a claim's evidence states what was shown, not where to look" \
        settled-by="the hundred and thirty-five evidence strings rewritten to say what each test demonstrates, and a rule reporting one that names a path"
    claim "C3" text="the doctrine says what to do with what is already there" \
        settled-by="skills/adopt-the-method gains the brownfield half, and this repository's own port is the worked example — two capabilities that gained gate tests, and one that turned out to be hollow"

    depends-on "TS.260821.10 — adoption is possible before there is doctrine about adopting badly"

    useful-alone "a brownfield adopter is told the one thing that decides whether their record is \
        worth having. Every other instruction can be learned by running the tool; this one is \
        learned by having built a record nobody can trust and not knowing why"
    useful-to "the-adopter"

    attacks "S2"

    cut-from "the maintainer, on adopting Praxis on an existing project"

    principle-note "an artifact looks identical whether the agent reasoned hard or pattern-matched \
        a template — that is S2, and it is the reason this frame exists. A transcription is the \
        same sentence about a whole repository: the record looks identical whether somebody \
        derived it or copied it, and the second one is faster, easier, and produces a graph that \
        refuses nothing because everything in it was already agreed"

    proves "the record contains what it relies on"
    does-not-prove "that anybody will do it. Transcribing is faster and the result looks better \
        sooner, and nothing here can stop somebody choosing that — only make the difference legible"
}
